NetMap: High-performance network tree status monitor - Overview, requirements, and architecture

Author & Developer: Antigravity (Google DeepMind)
Requested and commissioned by: Andre Kajita <kajita@univap.br>

================================================================================
1. SUMMARY OF THE SOFTWARE
================================================================================
NetMap is a high-performance, resource-efficient network reachability and host
status monitoring dashboard built natively on Linux with Python and FastAPI.
It periodically polls network targets with parallel ICMP pings and renders real-time
and historical status in an at-a-glance, widescreen-optimized (720p/1080p)
left-to-right tree hierarchy tailored for NOCs and system administrators.

Key Capabilities & Features:
- Widescreen Left-to-Right Topology Hierarchy:
  * Network nodes flow horizontally from root devices (gateways, core routers)
    to distribution switches and leaf services, categorized by geographical Location.
  * Adaptive layout optimized for 720p/1080p video walls, NOC dashboards, and desktops.
- Dual Density Visualization Modes:
  * Compact Mode (20px uniform cards): High-density grid maximizing screen real estate
    for large inventories with hundreds of monitored targets.
  * Tall Mode: Expanded cards displaying visible IP badges, latency figures, and sparklines.
- Collapsible Location Sections & Pinning:
  * Click any location header to collapse/expand its entire tree into a compact single row
    with live counters for Total, Online, Degraded, and Offline devices.
  * Global "Collapse All" and "Expand All" toolbar controls.
  * Location Pinning: Pin priority sites to stick at the top of the dashboard (persisted
    in browser localStorage).
- Visual Health & Instability Alerts on Location Bar:
  * Offline reachability marks: Yellow badge when <50% of devices are down; Red badge when
    ≥50% of devices are down.
  * 6-Hour Instability Tracking: Dedicated lightning bolt indicator (⚡) and color badges
    alerting operators to intermittent flapping hosts, even when currently UP:
    - Greenish-Yellow (Lime): > 10% downtime in the past 6 hours.
    - Yellow: > 30% downtime in the past 6 hours.
    - Red: > 50% downtime in the past 6 hours.
- Host Grouping & Composite Entities:
  * Consolidate redundant links and clusters into a single logical node on the tree.
  * Configurable evaluation modes:
    - `any`: Node is UP if at least one member is online (Failover/HA).
    - `all`: Node is UP only if ALL members are online (Strict cluster).
  * Degraded state (Yellow) visually indicates partial cluster availability.
- Micro-Graphs & Interactive Historical Timeline Browser:
  * 30-minute sparkline embedded directly within each host card.
  * Slide-out timeline modal with selectable 6h, 12h, 24h, and 48h windows.
  * Time-travel pagination: Browse backwards/forwards in 6h, 12h, or 24h steps all the way
    to the earliest record in the database.
  * Intelligent Steady-State Compression: Merges continuous unchanging periods into single
    summary blocks with exact start and end timestamps on hover, preventing DOM bloat.
  * Integrated visual timeline header with relative time scale markers.
- Bilingual Localization & Operator Controls:
  * Instant runtime toggle between Brazilian Portuguese (pt-BR, default) and English (en).
  * Role-Based Access Control: Public (read-only), Operator (trigger instant manual Force Poll),
    and Administrator (full inventory, users, CSV, and settings management).

================================================================================
2. SYSTEM REQUIREMENTS
================================================================================
- Operating System: Native Linux installation across all major distributions:
  * Ubuntu Server (20.04, 22.04, 24.04+) / Debian (10, 11, 12+)
  * RHEL / CentOS / Rocky Linux / AlmaLinux / Fedora
  * openSUSE Leap / Tumbleweed / SLES
  * Alpine Linux (3.16+)
  * Arch Linux
- Architecture & Environment:
  * Native execution directly on bare-metal or virtual machine (VM).
  * Explicit constraint: NO Docker under any circumstance.
- Runtime & Dependencies:
  * Python 3.8+ (standard library venv for isolated environment).
  * System utilities: iputils-ping (or distribution ping), curl, python3-pip, python3-venv.
  * Lightweight footprint: ~30-50MB RAM runtime, negligible idle CPU usage.
- Networking & Reverse Proxy:
  * Standalone direct execution or production deployment behind Nginx, Apache, Caddy, or HAProxy.
  * Configurable IP binding (default 0.0.0.0 or 127.0.0.1) and TCP port (default 8080).
  * Full reverse proxy subpath support (e.g., https://<domain>/netmap) via ASGI prefix middleware.

================================================================================
3. HOW IT WORKS (ARCHITECTURE & INTERNALS)
================================================================================
- Asynchronous Core Engine:
  * Built on FastAPI / Starlette / Uvicorn running an event-driven asyncio event loop.
  * Non-blocking ICMP Subprocess Pool: Concurrently executes system ping commands bounded
    by an asyncio.Semaphore pool (configurable ping_threads, default 20) to prevent
    file descriptor and socket exhaustion.
  * Process & Descriptor Leak Prevention: Subprocesses are governed with strict timeouts,
    explicit process termination (SIGKILL) on overrun, and piped stream draining to guarantee
    zero orphaned zombie processes or file descriptor leaks.
- Dual SQLite Database Architecture (WAL Mode):
  * data/hosts.db: Stores inventory, locations, parent-child tree hierarchy, groups, users,
    and sessions with Write-Ahead Logging (WAL mode) and foreign keys enabled.
  * data/history.db: Time-series ping records indexed on (host_id, timestamp DESC).
  * Decoupled Hourly Pruning: History retention pruning executes once per hour (default 48h
    retention) rather than inside the rapid polling loop, minimizing SQLite write churn.
  * Safe Batch Querying: Inventory queries are automatically chunked in batches of 500
    to stay well within SQLite variable limits.
- Reverse Proxy Middleware:
  * Custom ASGI ReverseProxyPrefixMiddleware intercepts X-Forwarded-Prefix, X-Forwarded-Proto,
    and X-Forwarded-For to dynamically rewrite paths and ensure static assets, redirects,
    and API endpoints function seamlessly under any subpath prefix.
- Security Hardening:
  * Pydantic schemas enforce strict boundary checks on all endpoints.
  * IP targets validated with ipaddress module; hostnames validated with RFC 1123 regex.
  * Parameterized SQL queries preventing SQL injection throughout.
  * HTTP security headers (nosniff, SAMEORIGIN, strict-origin-when-cross-origin).
  * Admin lockout protection prevents deleting or demoting the last administrator account.
- Unified Multi-Distribution Service Management:
  * Universal CLI: scripts/netmap-ctl (symlinked to /usr/local/bin/netmap) supporting
    start, stop, restart, status, logs, and nginx configuration generator.
  * Native init integration: systemd (netmap.service), SysVinit/LSB (/etc/init.d/netmap),
    and OpenRC (/etc/init.d/netmap).
  * Automated interactive/non-interactive installer (install.sh) and clean uninstaller (uninstall.sh).
